\description{
Converts the continuous trait history(ies) mapped on a phylogeny into a stochastic character map of class \code{"simmap"}
with discrete states obtained by cutting the trait values into bins (e.g., \code{"low"}, \code{"medium"}, \code{"high"}).

The output can be used as a \code{simmap} in \code{\link[=graft_missing_taxa]{phylograftR::graft_missing_taxa()}} and
\code{\link[=graft_missing_clades]{phylograftR::graft_missing_clades()}} to constrain the grafting of missing taxa
by the continuous trait (e.g., a taxon known to have a high body size can only be grafted on branches in the \code{"high"} state).

The function accepts a single contMap, typically built with \code{\link[phytools:contMap]{phytools::contMap()}},
or a list of contMaps typically obtained from continuous stochastic mapping approach such as the one featured in the \code{contsimmap} R package.
\strong{A single \code{contMap} gives a single \code{simmap}.} A list of \code{contMap}s, typically covering the uncertainty of the reconstruction
(e.g., one \code{contMap} per stochastic history of a continuous trait), gives a \strong{\code{multiSimmap}}, with \strong{one \code{simmap} per \code{contMap}}.
}
\details{
\subsection{Principle}{

A \code{contMap} stores the trait history as a series of short segments along each branch,
with ancestral node values typically estimated from a maximum likelihood reconstruction under a Brownian motion (BM) model
and linearly interpolated along the branches.

During discretization, each segment is assigned to the bin its value falls in, and consecutive segments of the same bin are merged.
\strong{A value equal to a cut point belongs to the bin above it.} The result is a \code{simmap} (as in \code{\link[phytools:make.simmap]{phytools::make.simmap()}}) where the states are the bins.
}

\subsection{Cutting the trait values}{

\strong{Provide exactly one of the three arguments:}
\itemize{
\item \code{breaks}: the cut points are chosen by the user.
\item \code{n_bins}: uses bins of equal width over the range of the tip values.
\item \code{probs}: uses bins defined by quantiles of the tip values.
}

\code{n_bins} and \code{probs} are computed on the tip values (averaged across the \code{contMap}s of a list).
By default, they are read from the \code{contMap}s, which store the trait values on a finite scale of colors (from 0 to 1000 in \code{phytools::contMap()}), so they approximate the observed data.
\strong{Provide the observed values with \code{tip_values} to use them instead} (see below).
}

\subsection{Tip values}{

A \code{contMap} stores the trait history as a series of short segments along each branch, with the value of each segment interpolated from the estimated values at the nodes.
The value associated to a segment is a single value for the whole segment, on a finite scale of colors. As a result, the value of the last segment of a terminal branch
is \strong{nearly, but not identical to the observed value at the tip}. With the default settings, the function extrapolates the tip value from the last segments, which is only an approximate.
The difference is usually small compared to the range of the trait, but it can move the tip of a taxon with a value close to a cut point into the neighboring state,
or shift the cut points obtained from \code{n_bins} or \code{probs}.

To avoid this, provide the \strong{observed tip values} with \code{tip_values}: they are then used to compute the cut points, and \strong{each tip is set in the state of its observed value},
so that the tips of the \code{simmap}s are in the same states as the observed data converted with \code{\link[=convert_values_to_states]{phylograftR::convert_values_to_states()}}.
This is recommended whenever the tip data used to build the \code{contMap}(s) are available.
When the observed state of a tip differs from the state of the interpolated segments just before it, the terminal branch ends with a short segment in the state of the observed value
(the segment at the tip is never merged away, see \code{min_segment_proportion}).

The values of the internal branches are reconstructed and are expected to shrink towards the intermediate values under a BM process
(or towards one specific direction if a directional model was used to fit the \code{contMap}s), because ancestral values are averaged over their descendants.
As a consequence, bins defined from the tips will contain a smaller share of the internal branches for the extreme states than for the intermediate states.

\code{breaks} must be interior cut points: they must lie within the range of the trait values, i.e.,
between the minimum and the maximum of the values of all the \code{contMap}s. \strong{A cut point beyond this range is an error.}
A cut point equal to the minimum or the maximum is ignored with a warning, because it would define an empty bin.

The cut points must be in increasing order and different values. This is checked for the three arguments, including the cut points derived from \code{n_bins} and \code{probs}
(e.g., many tips sharing the same value can give identical quantiles). Unsorted cut points are sorted, and duplicated values are removed, with an informative warning.

\strong{The number of \code{states} must be equal to the number of bins} (the number of cut points + 1, after the cut points equal to the boundaries were ignored).
Removing cut points reduces the number of bins, so \code{states} must match the number of bins left.
}

\subsection{Short segments}{

When using \code{contMap}s derived from continuous stochastic mapping processes such as the one featured in the \code{contsimmap} R package,
trait values can wander around a cut point: the trait evolutionary history can crosses the threshold several times over short distances,
resulting in tiny segments alternating states.
\strong{Segments shorter than \code{min_segment_proportion} times the depth of the tree are merged}, from the shortest to the longest, into their longest neighbour on the same branch.
A branch shorter than the minimum length gets a single state: the one with the longest total duration, or the state of the tip for a terminal branch.
On a terminal branch, the segment at the tip is never merged away to ensure tip states matched with observed tip data.
Setting \code{min_segment_proportion = 0} keeps all the crossings.

This is not an issue for \code{contMap} featuring linear interpolation of ancestral trait values as in \code{\link[phytools:contMap]{phytools::contMap()}},
since trait values can only vary monotonously along branches (i.e., decrease, increase, or remain stable, but not a combination of two),
thus cannot cross each cut point multiple times.
}

\subsection{Cautions for interpretation}{
\itemize{
\item The resulting \code{simmap}s is/are a discretization of the reconstructed trait to constrain the grafting of missing taxa.
\strong{It is not a stochastic map of a discrete trait}: the number of transitions depends on the resolution of the \code{contMap} and on \code{min_segment_proportion}
and should not be interpreted as a rate of evolution of discrete states.
\item Nodes with reconstructed values close to a cut point have uncertain states. Providing a list of \code{contMap}s
covering the uncertainty (see below) is the way to account for it, as each grafted phylogeny can then use a different \code{simmap}.
\item The states depend on the cut points. A taxon whose allowed states are defined by a threshold can have different eligible branches
if the threshold is moved. Ideally, you would want to compare several sets of cut points before relying on the results (see \code{vignette("continuous_data")}).
\item As for the use of \code{simmap} derived from discrete states, constraining the grafting by a trait that will then be analyzed
(e.g., the association of the trait with diversification) biases the results towards the constraint. Interpret such analyses accordingly.
}
}

\subsection{Accounting for uncertainty in trait estimates: continuous stochastic mapping}{

A single \code{contMap} is a point estimate. To cover the uncertainty of the reconstruction, provide a list of \code{contMap}s, each one built from one possible history of the trait.
For example, the \code{contsimmap} R package samples histories of a continuous trait on a phylogeny, and the function \code{\link[deepSTRAPP:convert_contsimmap_to_contMaps]{deepSTRAPP::convert_contsimmap_to_contMaps()}}
converts them into a list of \code{contMap}s (see Examples). The output of this function is then a \strong{\code{multiSimmap}}, that can be used in the grafting functions like any \code{multiSimmap},
to account for uncertainty in the ancestral trait reconstructions.

The function \code{deepSTRAPP::prepare_trait_data()} is the \strong{recommended way} to obtain such a list of \code{contMap}s: with \code{run_stochastic_maps = TRUE}, it fits the evolutionary model(s) (selected by AICc),
runs the continuous stochastic mapping with \code{contsimmap::make.contsimmap()} and converts the maps into \code{contMap}s in a single call, with additional options to fine-tune the process (see Examples).
The example below uses \code{contsimmap} directly to credit the authors of the continuous stochastic mapping approach and to show the steps usde internally by \code{deepSTRAPP::prepare_trait_data()}.
}
}
\examples{
# --- Prepare data --- #

# Load backbone phylogeny
data(toy_backbone)

# Simulate a continuous trait on the backbone phylogeny under a Brownian Motion
# and interpolate its ML estimates with phytools::contMap()
tip_data <- phytools::fastBM(toy_backbone,
                          a = 0,  # Ancestral value at the root
                          sig2 = 1) # Evolutionary rate as instantaneous variance of the BM process
my_contMap <- phytools::contMap(tree = toy_backbone, x = tip_data, plot = FALSE)
plot(my_contMap, legend = FALSE)

# --- Discretize using quantiles of tip values --- #

# Convert into simmap of three states using quantiles of the tip values
my_simmap_1 <- convert_contMap_to_simmap(my_contMap, probs = c(1/3, 2/3),
                                       states = c("low", "medium", "high"))
attr(my_simmap_1, "breaks")

# Check the distribution of states across tips
tip_states <- phytools::getStates(my_simmap_1, type = "tips")
table(tip_states)

# --- Use the observed tip values --- #

# The contMap only approximates the values at the tips (interpolation within segments).
# Provide the observed tip values to compute the cut points
# and to set the state of each tip from the data
my_simmap_1b <- convert_contMap_to_simmap(my_contMap, probs = c(1/3, 2/3),
                                          states = c("low", "medium", "high"),
                                          tip_values = tip_data)
# Cut points computed from the observed tip values
attr(my_simmap_1b, "breaks")
quantile(tip_data, probs = c(1/3, 2/3))

# Tips are in the state of their observed values
tip_states_b <- phytools::getStates(my_simmap_1b, type = "tips")
converted_tips_b <- unlist(convert_values_to_states(tip_data, simmap = my_simmap_1b))
all(tip_states_b == converted_tips_b[names(tip_states_b)])

# Plot the resulting simmap
plot(my_simmap_1, colors = c(low = "blue", medium = "grey", high = "red"),
     fsize = 0.7, ftype = "i")

# --- Discretize using cut points --- #

# Use two interior cut points defined by the user to obtain three states
my_simmap_2 <- convert_contMap_to_simmap(my_contMap, breaks = c(-1, 1),
                                         states = c("low", "medium", "high"))

# Plot the resulting simmap
plot(my_simmap_2, colors = c(low = "blue", medium = "grey", high = "red"),
     fsize = 0.7, ftype = "i")

# --- Discretize using bins of equal width --- #

# Use bins of equal width to assign states
my_simmap_3 <- convert_contMap_to_simmap(my_contMap, n_bins = 3,
                                         states = c("low", "medium", "high"))

# Plot the resulting simmap
plot(my_simmap_3, colors = c(low = "blue", medium = "grey", high = "red"),
     fsize = 0.7, ftype = "i")


# --- Discretize multiple continuous stochastic maps --- #

\dontrun{ # Requires one package not available on CRAN
## The R packages 'deepSTRAPP' (on CRAN) and 'contsimmap' (not on CRAN)
## are needed for this example to work.
if (requireNamespace("deepSTRAPP", quietly = TRUE)
    && requireNamespace("contsimmap", quietly = TRUE))
{
 # Use phytools::anc.ML() to extract the evolutionary rate = sigma^2
 BM_fit <- phytools::anc.ML(tree = toy_backbone,
                            x = tip_data,
                            model = "BM")
 # Extract evolutionary rate = sigma^2
 sigma2 <- BM_fit$sig2

 # Simulate 100 continuous stochastic maps for the same trait data and BM model
 my_contsimmap <- contsimmap::make.contsimmap(
     tree = toy_backbone,
     trait.data = tip_data,
     Xsig2 = list(sigma2),
     nsim = 100)
 dim(my_contsimmap) # 58 edges, 1 trait, 100 simulations

 # Convert contsimmap output into a list of contMaps
 my_contMaps_list <- deepSTRAPP::convert_contsimmap_to_contMaps(
     contsimmap = my_contsimmap, verbose = TRUE)

 # Convert multiple contMaps at once into corresponding simmaps
 # using quantiles of the tip values
 my_multiSimmap <- convert_contMap_to_simmap(my_contMaps_list, probs = c(1/3, 2/3),
                                             states = c("low", "medium", "high"),
                                             tip_values = tip_data) # Use the observed tip values

 # Plot the resulting simmaps describing different evolutionary histories simulated
 # from the same evolutionary process conditioned to the tip data and phylogeny,
 # therefore accounting for uncertainty in ancestral trait estimates.
 plot(my_multiSimmap[[1]], colors = c(low = "blue", medium = "grey", high = "red"),
     main = "simmap n\u00B01", fsize = 0.7, ftype = "i")
 plot(my_multiSimmap[[2]], colors = c(low = "blue", medium = "grey", high = "red"),
      main = "simmap n\u00B02", fsize = 0.7, ftype = "i")
 plot(my_multiSimmap[[3]], colors = c(low = "blue", medium = "grey", high = "red"),
     main = "simmap n\u00B03", fsize = 0.7, ftype = "i")

 # --- Alternative: use deepSTRAPP::prepare_trait_data() (recommended) --- #

 # The steps above (fit of the BM model, simulation of the continuous stochastic maps
 # with contsimmap::make.contsimmap(), and conversion into a list of contMaps)
 # can be run at once with deepSTRAPP::prepare_trait_data().

 # The function offers additional options to fine-tune the process,
 # such as the comparison of several evolutionary models (selected by AICc),
 # the resolution of the contMaps, and the number of simulations.
 # It can also output directly both the interpolated point estimate as in phytools::contMap(),
 # and the set of stochastic continuous maps simulated with contsimmap::make.contsimmap().

 # Not to run: it replaces the steps above and returns the same type of output (a list of contMaps).
 # my_trait_data <- deepSTRAPP::prepare_trait_data(
 #     tip_data = tip_data,
 #     trait_data_type = "continuous",
 #     phylo = toy_backbone,
 #     seed = 1234, # Set seed for reproducibility
 #     evolutionary_models = "BM", # Evolutionary model(s) to fit
 #     run_stochastic_maps = TRUE, # Run continuous stochastic mapping to account for uncertainty
 #     nb_simulations = 100, # Number of stochastic maps
 #     plot_map = FALSE)
 #
 # my_contMap <- my_trait_data$contMap # Point estimate: a single contMap
 # my_contMaps_list <- my_trait_data$contMaps # List of contMaps from the stochastic maps
 #
 # my_multiSimmap <- convert_contMap_to_simmap(my_contMaps_list, probs = c(1/3, 2/3),
 #                                             states = c("low", "medium", "high"),
 #                                             tip_values = tip_data)
}
}

